# FigDown — figures as text. Spec: https://github.com/FigDown/figdown

figdown 0.1 table
title "VXLAN Overview — MAC-in-UDP encapsulation"

# ── Why this figure is tables, not pinned nodes ─────────────────────
# What a packet-header figure MEANS is the on-the-wire ORDER of the
# fields. An earlier revision drew each field as a free `node` and put
# the order into `pin` x-coordinates only: strip the layout lines and
# the order was gone (meaning-in-geometry, which `MEANING-RECOVERY-SOURCE` forbids).
# In a `table` the order IS the model: genres/table.md, "Semantic
# model" — the logical grid is derived from the declared rows and cell
# order, never from rendered geometry. So the byte order survives with
# ZERO layout lines. This document contains no flow/rank/pin/
# routing/path line and no `layout` zone at all, so the `GUI-WRITEBACK-STRUCTURE` strip test
# is a no-op on it. This is also what guide/authoring.md's pitfall table
# prescribes: a byte-block header sequence with no bit scale belongs in
# a single-row `table`, not in a `bitfield` and not in free nodes.

# ── Semantic classes (`MEANING-RECOVERY-SOURCE`: the meaning rides on the element) ────────
class outer  "Outer header added by the ingress VTEP (50B total)" fill=#fcd34d
class vni    "VLAN ID 12-bit → VNI 24-bit: the identifier VXLAN widens" fill=#fcd34d stroke=#ea580c
class inner  "Original L2 frame field — carried verbatim as VXLAN payload" fill=#bae6fd
class opaque "Upper-layer payload — opaque to the VTEP" fill=#9aa2ad
class oldfcs "Original FCS — dropped at encapsulation, not carried" fill=#93c5fd
class newfcs "New FCS — recomputed over the outer frame" fill=#bbf7d0 stroke=#16a34a

# ── Classical Ethernet frame ────────────────────────────────────────
# One data row: cell order left to right IS the on-the-wire byte order.
# The header tier groups the fields into header / data / trailer.
table eth "Classical Ethernet frame — cells left to right are on-the-wire order"
| Ethernet header |||| Data | Trailer |
|---------|---------|-----------|----------|------------------|--------|
| DMAC 6B | SMAC 6B | 802.1Q 4B | Etype 2B | Payload 46–1500B | FCS 4B |
cell (1,1) class=inner
cell (1,2) class=inner
cell (1,3) class=vni
cell (1,4) class=inner
cell (1,5) class=opaque
cell (1,6) class=oldfcs

# ── VXLAN-encapsulated frame ────────────────────────────────────────
# Same rule: cell order is byte order — Outer MAC, Outer IP, UDP,
# VXLAN, then the original L2 frame verbatim, then a recomputed FCS.
# NESTING, stated honestly: the table genre expresses containment as a
# header-tier span — columns 5–8 sit under "Original L2 frame", which
# says those four cells are one nested unit. That is one level only;
# the genre has no recursive sub-table, so "a frame inside a frame" is
# carried by the span plus the `inner` class meaning, not by a nested
# construct. The inner frame has no 802.1Q cell on purpose: the VTEP
# pops the tag and carries its value as the VNI.
# The VLAN→VNI relation used to be an `edge` between two field nodes.
# Edges cannot attach to table cells — cell anchors for edges are the
# unresolved open question `CELL-EDGE-ANCHORS` (spec §5) — so the relation is
# carried semantically by the shared `vni` class, whose label states
# the 12-bit → 24-bit widening and which marks both the 802.1Q cell of
# the classical frame and the VXLAN cell here.
table vxlan "VXLAN-encapsulated frame — cells left to right are on-the-wire order"
| Outer headers |||| Original L2 frame |||| New trailer |
|---------------|--------------|--------------|----------|---------|---------|----------|------------------|--------------|
| Outer MAC 14B | Outer IP 20B | Outer UDP 8B | VXLAN 8B | DMAC 6B | SMAC 6B | Etype 2B | Payload 46–1500B | FCS (new) 4B |
cell (1,1) class=outer
cell (1,2) class=outer
cell (1,3) class=outer
cell (1,4) class=vni
cell (1,5) class=inner
cell (1,6) class=inner
cell (1,7) class=inner
cell (1,8) class=opaque
cell (1,9) class=newfcs

# ── Supplementary knowledge ─────────────────────────────────────────
table overhead "Encapsulation overhead (MAC-in-UDP)"
| Segment    | Size | Notes                          |
|------------|:----:|--------------------------------|
| Outer MAC  | 14B  | new Ethernet header            |
| Outer IP   | 20B  | VTEP-to-VTEP                   |
| UDP        | 8B   | dst port 4789                  |
| VXLAN      | 8B   | carries the 24-bit VNI         |
| Total      | 50B  | added to every original frame  |
cell 5 highlight

table facts "Why VXLAN"
| Fact                    | Value                    |
|-------------------------|--------------------------|
| VLAN address space      | 12 bits (4K segments)    |
| VNI address space       | 24 bits (16M segments)   |
| Encapsulation           | MAC-in-UDP               |
